WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.780
Kia ora. Can I check you can hear and see me?

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.780
Good morning, Lizzie. Very good to have you here.

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.520
I'm Sam Athfield, chair of the Health Committee. We have members around the table.

4
00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:14.400
Welcome. It's very nice to have the opportunity to hear from you.

5
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.760
We have you here for 10 minutes. If you want to leave us time for questions,

6
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.580
you may. We have read your written

7
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:23.120
submission.

8
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.100
We will hand that time over to you now. Thank you.

9
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.200
Okay. Thank you.

10
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.521
Tena koutou, tena koutou, tena koutou katoa.

11
00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.280
Thank you for the opportunity to talk with you today.

12
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.180
I'm Lizzie Carey. I'm a qualified clinical prosthetist orthotist.

13
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.460
I provide prosthetic limbs for people living with limb difference or amputation,

14
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.660
helping them maintain mobility, independence, employment, and participation in

15
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.020
community life. I trained in the UK and moved to New Zealand in

16
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.940
2016 in response to a recognized skills shortage.

17
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.750
New Zealand has no domestic degree program for our profession, so we depend heavily

18
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.020
on clinicians who have trained overseas.

19
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.940
My experience includes working in the NHS, then NZALS, now known as Peki

20
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:04.340
Wāhanga,

21
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.490
and I have also worked for one of the largest global prosthetic manufacturers

22
00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.840
training clinicians across Australia and New Zealand, as well as delivering

23
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.540
training programs across Africa, South America, and Sri Lanka to improve access to

24
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.200
quality prosthetic care. There is a global shortage of prosthetist

25
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:22.420
orthotists, and due to New Zealand's lower wages and limited employer options, New

26
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.820
Zealand is struggling to obtain and maintain staffing levels required.

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.260
I've seen what New Zealand's prosthetic sector can achieve.

28
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.330
However, I've also seen many skilled clinicians leave, patients disengage from

29
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.000
care, and independent providers prevented from contributing fully because

30
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.940
publicly funded prosthetic care remains concentrated with one provider, Peki

31
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:41.360
Wāhanga.

32
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.520
Peki Wāhanga is a Crown entity, and I want to acknowledge that they provide

33
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.900
valuable and essential services to New Zealanders and have skilled clinicians.

34
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.650
I'm not asking for Peki Wāhanga to be dismantled.

35
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.080
The concern is the structure around it.

36
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.070
Since the National Artificial Limb Service, now known as Peki Wāhanga, developed as

37
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.820
post-World War service for veterans, publicly funded prosthetic care has been

38
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.820
delivered predominantly through a single-provider model.

39
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.100
There was a short period in the '90s when another provider held a regional

40
00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.530
contract. This was followed by a Health and Disability Commissioner's report in

41
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.680
2000 that stated that patients in that region were only offered a choice

42
00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.760
between providers for approximately five months before contracts were changed to

43
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.640
sole provider national contracts.

44
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.920
Meaningful patient choice has otherwise been largely absent for the past 80 years.

45
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.480
No single provider, particularly a Crown entity, that is also a service provider

46
00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.360
should be the only accessible funded option for almost every amputee.

47
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.300
Prosthetic care is not a single episode of care.

48
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.860
It's a lifelong clinical relationship.

49
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.220
Throughout the amputee's lifetime, their residual limb changes shape and size and

50
00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.320
may experience complications such as painful neuromas,

51
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:48.549
and bespoke prosthetics need to be replaced to match their current limb shape to

52
00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.680
prevent injury, pain, and wounds.

53
00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.760
Patients and their whānau have experienced a life-changing event that leaves them

54
00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:55.300
reliant on prosthetic care providers.

55
00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.430
When one organization controls almost every stage of that funded

56
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.760
stage of that relationship, the patient is placed in a uniquely

57
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.010
dependent position.

58
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.980
In most areas of healthcare, if trust breaks down or a person feels their needs are

59
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.140
not being met, they can seek another qualified clinician, and this is currently

60
00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:16.180
possible in physiotherapy and podiatry.

61
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.980
For most New Zealand amputees, that ordinary safeguard is not available.

62
00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.700
To paint the picture, I think it's important to hear the real-life experiences.

63
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.400
One of our patients, Jess Quinn, has given explicit consent to share her story with

64
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.300
you today. Jess had an amputation following cancer as a child,

65
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.540
and she was able to get really active as a young adult,

66
00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.960
jogging and even competing on "Dancing with the Stars." Unfortunately, she began

67
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.300
experiencing swelling in her limb, which was unable to be resolved with the only

68
00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.520
prosthetic provider in New Zealand, and after two and a half years of these issues

69
00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.140
and pain, she privately self-funded a prosthesis to be made in America,

70
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.380
which resolved these issues. However, after COVID, regular traveling to the US

71
00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:56.220
wasn't feasible, and over the next three years, multiple unsuccessful

72
00:03:56.260 --> 00:04:00.080
attempts to refit the prosthesis by Peki Wāhanga resulted in chronic pressure

73
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:00.460
sores.

74
00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.680
She was referred to have surgery.

75
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.720
Jess requested a second opinion from myself, and within three weeks of receiving

76
00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.940
the prosthetic I made for her, the pressure sores had completely healed, and the

77
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.460
surgery was avoided. In her words, "I could have gone through a pretty risky

78
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.041
surgery, in a sense I have no idea of what could have caused further

79
00:04:19.101 --> 00:04:22.299
complications. I was seeing every specialist under the sun.

80
00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.450
I think it just shows how desperate I got.

81
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.030
I spent a long time thinking this part of my life was over, the part where I could

82
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.070
move, work out, feel strong, and I can now." There are many similar stories to

83
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.060
her case of people being impacted by pain and lack of resolution, leading them to

84
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.610
avoid returning to the only funded service provider, and some resorting to

85
00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.440
abandoned prosthetic use. I'm not suggesting that every complex fitting

86
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.960
or clinical disagreement represents a failure. Prosthetic care is complex.

87
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.420
Residual limbs change, components sometimes fail, and achieving a successful fit

88
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.640
can require several attempts or approaches.

89
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:57.270
The complexity clearly points to the need for patients to have alternative options

90
00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.840
and for long-term support with a clinician who fits them best to be

91
00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.080
funded. Patients should have autonomy to choose their provider.

92
00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.220
Currently, the only pathway available to receiving a second opinion with a

93
00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.100
prosthetist is through arduous complaints process, which is only available to

94
00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.480
some ACC patients and is not guaranteed.

95
00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:18.310
Health-funded patients have to self-fund to see another provider.

96
00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.740
A complaints process is important, but it cannot substitute for

97
00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:26.660
provider choice. A patient shouldn't have to prove negligence or misconduct

98
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.820
before being allowed to receive care from another appropriately qualified

99
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:30.360
clinician.

100
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:35.660
Official Information Act requests from September 2025 showed that only 16 amputees

101
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.510
across the past five years were approved to see an alternative

102
00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:40.560
provider in New Zealand.

103
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.460
There are close to 5,000 prosthetic users in New Zealand, and that equates to

104
00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.620
0.3% of users, and only 11 patients were fully funded.

105
00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.600
This is not freedom of choice. This is exceptional circumstances.

106
00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:56.400
We face several connected issues in this industry: disparity in patient funding

107
00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.690
between ACC and Health New Zealand patients, absence of meaningful choice of

108
00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.020
provider or clinician, and high staff turnover and limited employment options.

109
00:06:04.492 --> 00:06:08.422
A sustainable workforce requires multiple employers, viable independent practice,

110
00:06:08.592 --> 00:06:12.212
opportunities for clinical specialization and professional development available

111
00:06:12.272 --> 00:06:15.312
across the profession, not just concentrated within one organization.

112
00:06:16.182 --> 00:06:19.491
And I want to be clear, I'm not asking for deregulation of the industry.

113
00:06:21.092 --> 00:06:23.612
Today, I'm asking the committee to recommend three practical actions.

114
00:06:24.272 --> 00:06:28.232
Firstly, a funded patient choice pathway, under which the appropriately qualified

115
00:06:28.252 --> 00:06:31.702
and independently accredited providers can deliver prosthetic care within national

116
00:06:31.732 --> 00:06:35.572
standards. Public funding should follow the patient and their assessed clinical

117
00:06:35.632 --> 00:06:39.292
needs to the accredited provider of their choice, rather than automatically

118
00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:41.652
attaching that person to one institution for life.

119
00:06:43.012 --> 00:06:46.172
Secondly, an immediate and transparent alternative provider pathway when the

120
00:06:46.192 --> 00:06:49.722
relationship has broken down, the patient has unresolved fit, safety, or functional

121
00:06:49.932 --> 00:06:53.552
concerns, a second clinical opinion is justified, or they do not consent to

122
00:06:53.592 --> 00:06:54.812
returning to a previous provider.

123
00:06:55.892 --> 00:06:59.852
And thirdly, a national consistent outcome reporting to an independent body

124
00:07:00.892 --> 00:07:04.092
that covers all providers and includes both patient-reported outcomes and

125
00:07:04.112 --> 00:07:07.702
functional outcome measures, falls and adverse events, complaints, transfers, and

126
00:07:07.752 --> 00:07:11.132
people disengaging from services. We can provide more details after this meeting.

127
00:07:12.332 --> 00:07:16.012
In closing, this is about building a wider, more resilient, and accountable system

128
00:07:16.052 --> 00:07:17.922
in which patients have a meaningful alternative.

129
00:07:18.572 --> 00:07:22.272
Giving amputees meaningful provider choice would protect their autonomy, strengthen

130
00:07:22.352 --> 00:07:25.712
clinical accountability, retain skilled clinicians in New Zealand, and help public

131
00:07:25.792 --> 00:07:27.492
funding achieve better long-term outcomes.

132
00:07:27.992 --> 00:07:30.812
And currently, we don't have the framework for prosthetists to develop their

133
00:07:30.852 --> 00:07:34.152
careers, and the current system makes it difficult for independent practitioners,

134
00:07:34.292 --> 00:07:36.632
like myself, to run their own sustainable business.

135
00:07:37.372 --> 00:07:40.792
I'd like to note that the president of NZOPA, our association, and some amputees

136
00:07:40.812 --> 00:07:43.152
have offered to be present in this call if you have questions that are more

137
00:07:43.192 --> 00:07:43.772
relevant to them.

138
00:07:46.692 --> 00:07:50.352
Lizzie, thank you very much for your submission today.

139
00:07:50.392 --> 00:07:53.482
The main message we're getting is that there's no practical

140
00:07:53.932 --> 00:07:57.652
accessible funding provider choice, leading to poorer outcomes

141
00:07:58.372 --> 00:08:01.592
for New Zealanders. And you've listed three

142
00:08:01.932 --> 00:08:05.552
recommendations there. I've seen Ayesha Vero has her hand up. Dr. Vero.

143
00:08:07.912 --> 00:08:11.072
Thank you, Lizzie, for a very clear and succinct presentation.

144
00:08:11.102 --> 00:08:12.092
I really appreciate it.

145
00:08:13.132 --> 00:08:14.472
The point you make about

146
00:08:15.532 --> 00:08:17.732
a patient's right to a second opinion is a good one.

147
00:08:17.792 --> 00:08:19.492
It's in the Code of Patient Rights.

148
00:08:20.172 --> 00:08:21.552
Can you explain,

